{"id": "reasoning-035348ae-b5a7-596d-be91-773a831eaf74-0", "sessionId": "cd5bdcf9-05e0-4243-d3ad-2b4390b79841", "turnId": "turn-7d9b8318e9bf4a5caf8fd8005ae407bd", "createdAt": 1792407958433, "updatedAt": 1792407958433, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "reasoning", "text": "Now I have a clear picture. Current state:\n\n**Done by prior run:**\n- qt.py: prep cache (`_SoftwareChunkPrep`, `_software_prep`, `_software_chunk_prep`, `_software_prep_signature`), queued pump (`_UpdateNotifier`, `_UpdateEvent`, `_start_update_pump` with postEvent notifier), queued hover (`_queue_hover(e, 0)` for GPU), QObject imported.\n- scheduler.py: notifier, pending, drain continuation repost.\n- retained_viewer.py: various preexisting perf changes (frame projection, etc.), but NOT: selection key narrowing, animation handle reuse.\n\n**To do (parent review first):**\n1. QObject import — already present. Verify via import smoke.\n2. Software cache prune: entries/chunks removed, animation clears, destroy — add pruning. Currently `_software_prep` never shrinks. Need:\n   - prune on remove_mesh (handle removal) — but removal flows through retained_viewer `_remove_entry`/`remove` calling `self._renderer.remove_mesh(handle)`. The prep cache lives on QtViewer (`self._software_prep`). Options: prune lazily in `_paint_software`/`_software_chunk_prep` — drop keys whose handle is no longer displayed. But \"Add bounded pruning and clear on destroy/clear so cache cannot accumulate obsolete meshes.\" Simplest robust approach:\n     - In `_software_chunk_prep`, before use, prune stale keys: keys whose handle id not in current display entries' handle ids (and animation entries). But computing that per chunk call is O(entries) — fine.\n     - Add explicit prune hooks: override `_mesh_changed`? Actually removal: `handle.remove()` → callback `_mesh_changed(handle, \"remove\")` → in retained_viewer, entry popped and `self._renderer.remove_mesh(handle)`. In qt.py `_mesh_changed` wraps super. I can add pruning in qt.py's `_mesh_changed` for change == \"remove\", and also chunk topology changes (remove_chunk → \"topology\" change; chunk ids change). And `_show_animation_frame` clears `_animation_handles` — old handles' preps become stale. And `destroy()` should clear `_software_prep`.\n     - Also bounded: cap cache size, e.g., prune oldest when exceeding a bound (say 512 entries) — use an OrderedDict-ish ordering. Simple: track insertion order via dict (Python dicts are ordered); when len > bound, evict oldest entries not currently... simplest: evict oldest until within bound.\n   - Implementation plan in qt.py:\n     - `_prune_software_prep(self, keep_ids=None, removed_ids=None)` helper.\n     - In `_mesh_changed`: after super, if change in (\"remove\", \"topology\"): drop keys for id(handle) not matching current chunk set; for \"remove\" drop all keys with key[0]==id(handle).\n     - In `_show_animation_frame` wrapper (qt.py): after super, prune keys whose handle id not in {id(h) for h in self._animation_handles} and not in live entries... Actually simpler: prune to currently displayed handles+chunks.\n     - In `_restore_live_renderer` wrapper: prune animation handles' entries.\n     - In `destroy()`: `self._software_prep.clear()`.\n     - Bounded: in `_software_chunk_prep`, after inserting, if len(self._software_prep) > _SOFTWARE_PREP_LIMIT (e.g. 1024), evict oldest.\n     - Also `_render_failed` fallback switches surface — prep cache still valid (world data camera-invariant) — fine.\n   - Careful: `_mesh_changed` in qt.py calls `self.make_current()` then super. For software backend make_current is a no-op unless closed. Add pruning after super call, guarded.\n\n3. Coalesced Qt notification with exception-safe continuation and atomic submit/close:\n   - Scheduler: add a `threading.Lock` for closed+put atomicity in submit, and close takes the same lock. `submit`: with lock: if closed: raise; put. Then notifier outside lock (notifier must not run under lock to avoid deadlock if notifier drains... postEvent is fine, but keep outside lock anyway).\n   - drain: exception-safe continuation: wrap `callback(*args, **kwargs)` in try/except, collect first exception, continue draining, re-raise first exception at end. That satisfies \"callback exception with more queued callbacks must still schedule remaining work\" and keeps observable failure (exception propagates to caller — in Qt customEvent it will print; in tests we can call drain directly).\n     - But wait: existing behavior — drain raised immediately on callback exception. Tests may rely on that (test_qt_deferred_failure?). With re-raise at end, the exception still propagates from drain — same observable contract for single failing callback. Good.\n   - Qt notifier coalescing: `_UpdateNotifier` gets a thread-safe pending flag (threading.Lock or just an atomic bool since Python has GIL — but use lock for correctness). notifier callable: \n     ```python\n     def wake():\n         with notifier._lock:\n             if notifier._pending:\n                 return\n             notifier._pending = True\n         QCoreApplication.postEvent(notifier, _UpdateEvent())\n     ```\n     customEvent: clear pending BEFORE draining (so submissions during drain post a new event):\n     ```python\n     with self._lock:\n         self._pending = False\n     owner._poll_updates()\n     ```\n     And drain's continuation repost: scheduler calls notifier() which posts only if not pending — since pending was cleared, it posts. Good.\n   - Exception in customEvent → _poll_updates → drain re-raises first exception. In PySide6, an exception in customEvent propagates into the C++ event loop; PySide6 prints the traceback (and since 6.x it may call qFatal? Actually PySide6 behavior: unhandled exception in a Python reimplemented event handler prints traceback and returns... In PySide6, exceptions in event handlers propagate to sys.excepthook; the process may abort with qFatal in some versions). Hmm, risk. Safer: in `_poll_updates`, catch exceptions from drain? But then \"observable failure\" — the review says \"Notifier errors must not strand accepted work without observable failure\" — that's about notifier() errors in submit, which are currently swallowed with `pass`. \"without observable failure\" — hmm, currently notifier errors are silently swallowed. Should they be recorded? The scheduler has no logger. The review: \"Notifier errors must not strand accepted work without observable failure.\" I read this as: if the notifier fails, the work must not be stranded (it stays queued, later drain executes), and the failure must be observable somehow. Currently swallowed silently — not observable. Options: re-raise notifier errors? But then submit crashes the producing thread — worse. Alternative: record the failure and re-raise on next drain? Complex. Or: after a notifier failure, mark `_notifier_failed = True` and... hmm.\n\n     Let me re-read: \"Submit/close race must not retain accepted work silently after close. Notifier errors must not strand accepted work without observable failure.\"\n     \n     Interpretation: (a) work accepted before close must not be silently dropped — close clears the queue, so work submitted before close is discarded... wait \"must not retain accepted work silently after close\" — i.e., after close, submit must raise (it does) and no work should silently remain queued (close drains). The race: submit checks closed=False, close runs and drains, submit puts → item stranded in queue after close with no one to drain it. Fix with lock.\n     \n     (b) notifier errors: if notifier() raises, the item is queued but no wakeup happened → stranded until next submit/drain. \"without observable failure\" — the failure should be observable. I think a reasonable approach: capture the notifier exception and re-raise it from the next `drain()` call on the owner thread (after draining remaining work), or store it and raise from `pending()`? Simplest observable: store the exception; `drain()` re-raises it after completing the drain (before/after processing?). Hmm, but drain is called by the notifier itself... if notifier failed, no event posted, drain won't be called by Qt. But tests can call drain directly. \n\n     Alternative simpler semantics: notifier failure → fall back: the item stays queued; record `_notifier_error`; the next `submit` attempt or `drain` raises it? That could break Tk path (no notifier → notifier is None → no error).\n\n     Actually maybe simpler: in submit, if notifier raises, we keep the item queued AND re-raise? No — \"a failing hook never removes the queued item, so a later drain still executes it\" — the docstring says failure-safe. Re-raising would crash the worker thread that submitted — the review says \"Notifier errors must not strand accepted work without observable failure\". If we re-raise, the work is still stranded (no wakeup) but failure is observable. Hmm, but crashing the submitter is bad.\n\n     Middle ground: record the exception in `self._notifier_error`; expose it via a method `take_notifier_error()` or have `drain()` raise it after draining. I'll implement: submit catches exception, stores first error in `self._notifier_error` (if not already set). `drain()` at the end: after processing, if `self._notifier_error` set, clear it and raise it (chained). This makes the failure observable on the owner thread at the next drain without stranding work (drain still executed everything). Also `pending()` unaffected.\n\n     Hmm wait, but if notifier fails, there's no wakeup → drain never called by Qt → work stranded anyway until next submit (whose notifier may also fail...). But the contract \"a later drain still executes it\" holds. The observable failure: drain raises the notifier error. I think that's a defensible, testable design. Let me also make the Qt notifier itself robust: postEvent essentially never fails.\n\n     Actually, let me reconside