{"id": "reasoning-1c434715-a7c0-507f-9d96-9a88dcb9e1c9-0", "sessionId": "cd5bdcf9-05e0-4243-d3ad-2b4390b79841", "turnId": "turn-7d9b8318e9bf4a5caf8fd8005ae407bd", "createdAt": 1792408124844, "updatedAt": 1792408124844, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "reasoning", "text": "Wait — replacement 2 was a no-op replace (same string) — I included it just to assert presence; fine.\n\nNow the prep cache pruning. Add:\n1. A `_prune_software_prep` method.\n2. Call at start of `_paint_software`.\n3. Explicit drop in `_mesh_changed` wrapper for \"remove\" and \"topology\".\n4. Clear in `destroy()`.\n5. Bound in `_software_chunk_prep` (move-to-end + evict oldest beyond limit).\n6. Prune after `_show_animation_frame` and `_restore_live_renderer` wrappers (drop keys for handles no longer displayed).\n\nThe qt.py wrappers:\n```python\n    def _mesh_changed(self, handle, change):\n        self.make_current()\n        return super()._mesh_changed(handle, change)\n\n    def _restore_live_renderer(self):\n        self.make_current()\n        return super()._restore_live_renderer()\n\n    def _show_animation_frame(self, index):\n        self.make_current()\n        return super()._show_animation_frame(index)\n```\n\nNew:\n```python\n    def _mesh_changed(self, handle, change):\n        self.make_current()\n        result = super()._mesh_changed(handle, change)\n        if change in (\"remove\", \"topology\"):\n            # Removed meshes and vanished chunks must not retain world\n            # arrays in the software preparation cache.\n            self._prune_software_prep()\n        return result\n\n    def _restore_live_renderer(self):\n        self.make_current()\n        result = super()._restore_live_renderer()\n        self._prune_software_prep()\n        return result\n\n    def _show_animation_frame(self, index):\n        self.make_current()\n        result = super()._show_animation_frame(index)\n        self._prune_software_prep()\n        return result\n```\n\n`_prune_software_prep`:\n```python\n    def _prune_software_prep(self):\n        \"\"\"Drop cached preparations that no longer match displayed chunks.\n\n        Removed handles, vanished chunks and superseded animation frames\n        must not retain world arrays; the cache only keeps chunks that the\n        current display entries still reference.\n        \"\"\"\n        cache = self._software_prep\n        if not cache:\n            return\n        keep = set()\n        for entry in self._display_entries().values():\n            handle = entry[\"handle\"]\n            if handle.removed or not handle.visible:\n                continue\n            if entry.get(\"appearance\", {}).get(\"depth_only\"):\n                continue\n            keep.add((id(handle), None))\n            for chunk_id, _mesh in handle.chunks:\n                keep.add((id(handle), chunk_id))\n        for key in tuple(cache):\n            if key not in keep:\n                del cache[key]\n```\n\nWait — chunk_id hashable: yes (Hashable). But `keep` uses tuples (id, chunk_id) — hashable. OK.\n\nHmm — one concern: `_display_entries()` — during animation returns `_animation_entries`. After `_restore_live_renderer`, returns `_entries`. Good.\n\nBound in `_software_chunk_prep`: after `self._software_prep[key] = prep`:\n```python\n        cache = self._software_prep\n        cache.pop(key, None)\n        cache[key] = prep\n        while len(cache) > _SOFTWARE_PREP_LIMIT:\n            cache.pop(next(iter(cache)))\n```\nDefine module constant `_SOFTWARE_PREP_LIMIT = 1024`.\n\ndestroy(): add `self._software_prep.clear()` — where? After `attempt(\"cancel_hover\", ...)`. Add `attempt(\"clear_prep_cache\", self._software_prep.clear)`.\n\nAlso `_render_failed` fallback: creates new _SoftwareResources; prep cache entries still keyed by handle ids — handles unchanged, still valid. Fine.\n\nNow line endings: the prep-cache region was written by prior run — check endings. The `_software_chunk_prep` code I read showed \\r\\n in the read output... Actually the read output showed `\\r\\n` for the whole file uniformly? The first read showed content with \\r\\n everywhere. But cat -A showed some lines without ^M. Mixed. My python approach handles it — I'll write replacements with \\r\\n and if assertion fails, try \\n. Let me write a helper that tries both.", "summary": []}
