er.py, but reading is fine.\n\nAlternatively: only remove live entries that are actually in the renderer. There's no \"contains\" API probably. Simplest: in reuse path, call `self._renderer.remove_mesh(entry[\"handle\"])` for live entries each frame — mirrors old behavior exactly. Cost: dict pop per live entry per frame — negligible vs old full rebuild.\n\nBut careful — software `_SoftwareResources.remove_mesh` bumps mask_revision → invalidates prep cache every frame! That would defeat the software prep cache during animation. Hmm. In the OLD code path, remove_mesh for live entries was called every frame too, plus add_mesh for animation handles every frame (also bumps mask_revision). So old software behavior already invalidated prep every frame. With reuse, if I call remove_mesh on live entries every frame, mask_revision bumps → prep cache invalidated every frame → no software prep benefit during animation. But correctness first; and animation frames change geometry anyway (generations in signature) → prep invalidated regardless. So no loss.\n\nActually wait — is calling remove_mesh for a handle never added safe? For _SoftwareResources: pops from dicts, no error. For GPU renderer: check. Let me grep remove_mesh in gpu/renderer.py.\n\nAlso — does `_display_entries` include live entries during animation? No — returns `_animation_entries` when `_animation_frame_active`. So the paint loop won't paint live entries. But GPU renderer renders its own groups. So to preserve exact GPU behavior, reuse path must remove live-entry groups from renderer each frame. Let me check renderer.remove_mesh semantics.", "summary": []}
