{"id": "reasoning-bb464b2d-8768-52a2-8174-3b6a789fe749-0", "sessionId": "cd5bdcf9-05e0-4243-d3ad-2b4390b79841", "turnId": "turn-7d9b8318e9bf4a5caf8fd8005ae407bd", "createdAt": 1792408333850, "updatedAt": 1792408333850, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "reasoning", "text": "Now rewrite tests/test_qt_update_pump.py for the queued-notification contract. Design of the fake Pump:\n\n```python\nclass Pump(QObject):\n    _start_update_pump = QtViewer._start_update_pump\n    _poll_updates = QtViewer._poll_updates\n    after_cancel = QtViewer.after_cancel\n    destroy = QtViewer.destroy\n    _cancel_hover = QtViewer._cancel_hover\n    def __init__(self):\n        super().__init__()\n        self._closed=False;self._update_poll_id=None;self._timers=set()\n        self._update_scheduler=ViewerScheduler();self.redraws=0\n        self._hover_timer=None;self._pending_hover=None\n        self._update_notifier=None;self._software_prep={}\n        self._renderer=SimpleNamespace(release=lambda:None)\n    def redraw(self):\n        if not self._closed:self.redraws+=1\n    def stop_animation(self):pass\n    def _release_gl(self):pass\n    def hide(self):pass\n```\n\ndestroy() references: counters getattr default; stop_animation; _closed=True; _cancel_hover; _software_prep.clear; _update_scheduler.close; timers; _release_gl; renderer.release; hide; deleteLater; _note_lifecycle getattr None. OK.\n\nTests:\n\n1. `test_idempotent_start_and_no_idle_polling`: start twice → same notifier object; no QTimer children; `pump.findChildren(QTimer)==[]`; notifier pending False.\n\n2. `test_worker_submission_runs_fifo_on_owner_through_posted_event`: submit 4 from worker thread; run event loop 20ms; seen == FIFO on owner thread; redraws==1 (one drain). Also coalescing: 4 submits → 1 event → 1 redraw.\n\n3. `test_drain_limit_retained_and_continuation_reposts`: submit 1025; start pump (initial drain processes 1024? wait — `_start_update_pump` calls `_poll_updates()` first, which drains 1024, then drain's continuation posts an event; then sets notifier; then `_poll_updates()` again drains the remaining 1. Hmm — the initial `_poll_updates` happens BEFORE the notifier is attached, so the continuation repost inside drain calls `self._notify()` with notifier None → no-op. Then after attaching, the second `_poll_updates` drains the last item. So after `_start_update_pump`, seen==1025, redraws==2. Then no pending events. Let me structure the test accordingly: after start, all 1025 executed.\n\n   Better test for continuation: attach notifier first via start with empty queue; then submit 1025 from owner thread (submit works from any thread; notifier posts event); run event loop: first event drains 1024, continuation posts second event, drains 1. seen==1025, redraws==2. And count events delivered: instrument by wrapping? We can count `wake` calls that posted: monkeypatch QCoreApplication.postEvent? Simpler: check `pump._update_notifier._pending is False` at end and redraws==2 proves two drains.\n\n4. `test_destroy_detaches_notifier_rejects_submissions_and_stale_event`: start; submit 'stale' from worker (posts event); destroy; run loop; seen==[]; submit raises RuntimeError; stale posted event delivered → customEvent sees owner._closed → returns without polling. Also `pump._update_scheduler._notifier is None`.\n\n5. `test_initial_callback_failure_does_not_arm_notifier`: submit fail; `_start_update_pump` raises ValueError; `_update_poll_id is None`; then start again → notifier armed and active... \"active\" for a notifier means pending flag; just assert `_update_poll_id is not None`.\n\n6. `test_worker_cannot_drain`: same as before.\n\n7. `test_coalesced_wakeups_single_event_for_burst`: submit 50 from worker; run loop; seen all 50; redraws==1 (single drain → one redraw). This proves coalescing (old design would also give 1 redraw with a single drain... but old design posted 50 events → 50 customEvent → 50 _poll_updates → first drains 50, rest drain 0 → redraws==1 too. Hmm — redraws doesn't distinguish. Count events: patch `QCoreApplication.postEvent`? We can count via notifier: add a counter by wrapping notifier.wake? The notifier is created inside _start_update_pump. We can wrap after creation: `wake = pump._update_notifier.wake; calls=[]; pump._update_notifier.wake = lambda: (calls.append(1), wake())` — but scheduler holds a reference to the bound method `notifier.wake` captured at set_notifier time. Rebinding attribute won't affect scheduler's captured reference. Instead monkeypatch qt.QCoreApplication.postEvent with a counting wrapper before starting the pump:\n\n```python\nposted=[]\nreal=qt.QCoreApplication.postEvent\ndef counting(receiver,event):\n    posted.append(1);return real(receiver,event)\nmonkeypatch.setattr(qt.QCoreApplication,'postEvent',static...\n```\nHmm — QCoreApplication.postEvent is a static method; monkeypatch.setattr(qt.QCoreApplication, 'postEvent', counting) — PySide6 may not allow setting attributes on the class (built-in type). setattr on PySide6 classes raises TypeError? PySide6 classes are mutable? Usually you can't monkeypatch built-in types. Alternative: patch `qt.QCoreApplication` with a fake object exposing postEvent? `_UpdateNotifier.wake` references the module-global `QCoreApplication` — monkeypatch.setattr(qt, 'QCoreApplication', FakeApp) where FakeApp.postEvent is a staticmethod that records and forwards to real. That works since wake looks up `QCoreApplication` in module globals at call time.\n\nSo test: monkeypatch qt.QCoreApplication with a shim; submit 50 from worker; assert posted count == 1 (coalesced); restore; run loop; all executed.\n\nActually careful: `_start_update_pump` doesn't call postEvent. The shim needs `postEvent` only. Let me implement:\n\n```python\nclass _AppShim:\n    def __init__(self,real):self.real=real;self.posts=[]\n    def postEvent(self,receiver,event):\n        self.posts.append(receiver);self.real.postEvent(receiver,event)\n```\nmonkeypatch.setattr(qt,'QCoreApplication',_AppShim(QCoreApplication)) — but the module also uses QCoreApplication elsewhere? In qt.py: `_defer_render_failure` uses QTimer; `QCoreApplication` used in _UpdateNotifier.wake only? grep: qt.py uses QCoreApplication only in wake (and import). OK.\n\n8. Exception-safe continuation at pump level: submit fail, submit ok; `_poll_updates` raises ValueError but ok was executed. Actually drain raises after processing both. Test: with pytest.raises(ValueError): pump._poll_updates(); assert seen==['ok'].\n\nNow scheduler tests (extend test_scheduler.py):\n- notifier called once per submit; failing notifier keeps item; error re-raised on next drain (and item executed).\n- drain continues after callback exception, raises first failure, remaining executed.\n- drain reposts notifier when limit leaves items (fake notifier counts calls).\n- atomic close/submit race: threads submit while close; after close, queue empty; submits that succeeded were drained... Actually test: spawn thread submitting in a loop until RuntimeError; main calls close concurrently; after join, `scheduler._queue.empty()` is True. And pending() False.\n- pending() helper.\n- close detaches notifier (no notifier call during close).\n\nNow test_performance_improvements.py:\n\nA. GPU hover (fake Hover-like owner with QTimer, QCoreApplication):\n- `test_gpu_hover_uses_bounded_nonrestarting_interval`: queue with restart=False, interval 16 → timer interval 16, single shot; second call while active does NOT restart (check remainingTime stays decreasing / or track start count). How to verify non-restarting: monkeypatch timer.start? Simpler: record `timer.remainingTime()` right after first call, sleep ~10ms (use QEventLoop with QTimer.singleShot(8, quit)), call again, assert remainingTime < initial remaining (i.e., it was NOT restarted). Timing-based but with generous margins: interval 16ms; after first start remaining ~16; wait 8ms; remaining ~8; second call; if restarted, remaining would be ~16 again; assert remaining < 12. Then run loop 30ms → delivered once with latest coords.\n- `test_software_hover_debounce_restarts`: queue (default), wait 60ms, queue again → remainingTime > 60 (restarted; interval 120). Then deliver → latest.\n- exact clicks: press cancels — already covered in responsiveness file; add: `_cancel_hover` then `_deliver_hover` → no read.\n\nB. Software prep cache reuse + invalidation + pruning (RetainedViewer + fake host + QImage, like the responsiveness test):\n- Build viewer with mesh; paint twice → `_software_chunk_prep` called once? Instrument: monkeypatch `qt.QtViewer._software_chunk_prep`? It's called as self._software_chunk_prep — bind counting wrapper on the instance: `v._software_chunk_prep = lambda *a: (calls.append(1), orig(*a))[1]`. Or simply check `len(v._software_prep)` and identity: paint twice, assert prep object identity same (cache hit) — count via wrapping.\n- Camera-only change (orbit) → still cache hit (same prep object).\n- Scalar change → new prep (signature includes generations).\n- Mask change (set_highlight) → new prep.\n- Section plane change → new prep.\n- Removal: handle.remove() → `_software_prep` no longer contains key (prune hook in _mesh_changed).\n- Chunk removal: handle.remove_chunk → prune drops chunk key.\n- destroy → cache cleared. (destroy on RetainedViewer fake? destroy is QtViewer method — the fake v is RetainedViewer... I can call `qt.QtViewer.destroy(v)`? destroy uses QWidget deleteLater... The fake in responsiveness test doesn't call destroy. For destroy-clear test, use the Pump fake (QObject) — pump._software_prep = {(1,None): object()}; pump.destroy(); assert empty. Good — put in pump test file or perf file with a small QObject fake.\n- Bound: create fake with >1024 entries? `_software_chunk_prep` bound test: simulate by calling the bound-eviction logic... simpler: unit-test the eviction by inserting 1025 preps via direct calls? Heavy. Alternative: test the eviction loop via a tiny fake: set `_SOFTWARE_PREP_LIMIT`? It's a module constant read at runtime — monkeypatch qt._SOFTWARE_PREP_LIMIT to 2, then paint a scene with 3 meshes → cache size <= 2. \n\nC. Animation reuse (RetainedViewer + fake host + fake renderer):\nNeed a fake renderer with add_mesh/remove_mesh counters. RetainedViewer.initialize_state sets `self._renderer`? Let me check what initialize_state sets for _renderer — line ~222 area. In the responsiveness test they set `v._renderer=qt._SoftwareResources()`. For animation tests, use qt._SoftwareResources too (it has add_mesh/remove_mesh/set_semantic_masks etc. needed by _apply_highlight_masks). Let me check what _apply_highlight_masks needs: renderer.set_semantic_masks, set_chunk_semantic_masks, set_chunk_pickable maybe. _SoftwareResources has those. \n\nFake host: `SimpleNamespace(make_current=lambda:None, framebuffer_size=lambda:(120,90), request_redraw=lambda:None)`.\n\nTest flow:\n```python\nv=RetainedViewer();v.initialize_state(width=120,height=90)\nv._renderer=qt._SoftwareResources();v._hud=qt._PainterHud()\nv._render_hud=lambda size:None\nv._host=fake_host\nhandle=v.add_mesh_arrays(mesh,tags='face',color='#123456',cull_backface=False)\nv.begin_animation_cache()\nv.capture_animation_frame()\nhandle.update_displacements(disp)  # frame 2 differs\nv.capture_animation_frame()\nv._show_animation_frame(0)\nfirst=tuple(v._animation_handles); adds0=v._renderer.geometry_uploads\nv._show_animation_frame(1)\nassert tuple(v._animation_handles)==first  # reuse\nassert v._renderer.geometry_uploads==adds0  # no re-add\nassert np.allclose(v._animation_handles[0].mesh.displacements, disp)\n```\nWait — `capture_animation_frame` captures `handle.mesh.owned_copy()` — displacements included. Frame 1 has no displacements; frame 2 has displacements → shape guard: None → allowed (None transition) → reuse with update_displacements(disp). \n\nAlso verify entries rebuilt: `v._animation_entries` keys same ids; entry appearance matches frame 2 item